# Auto-commit after code changes

When a coding task is finished and there are meaningful file changes:

1. **Always create a git commit** without waiting for the user to ask. This overrides the default “only commit when requested” habit for this project.
2. Follow the normal git safety protocol: never update git config; never force-push; never amend unless the usual amend conditions are met; never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, etc.).
3. Use a concise commit message focused on **why** (1–2 sentences). On Windows PowerShell, prefer:
   `git commit -m @" ... "@`
4. After a successful commit, **try `git push`** to `origin` when the branch tracks a remote. If the network fails, report that the commit is local and push can be retried later—do not block on push.
5. Skip empty commits when there is nothing to commit. If the only change is the rule/docs the user did not ask to commit as part of the task, still include related project fixes from that same turn.
6. Do not push `--force` to main/master.
